  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 AM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nThe forecast has continued to remain on track from the previous few \nforecasts. We expect drier conditions to continue through the \nweekend in South Texas outside of isolated seabreeze showers and \nthunderstorms. That is due to weak mid level ridging and a slightly \ndrier airmass that is parked over the region. We will likely still \nsee some activity over the Texas waters due to Hurricane Isaias as \nit moves north-northeast away from us. We will see those drier \nconditions across the region leading to much warmer temperatures \nacross S TX. Some residual energy from Hurricane Simon over in the \nPacific will look to creep into the region early next week with \nSouth Texas on the right flank of the area of low pressure. Ahead of \nthis onshore flow will return to the region allowing for deep \nmoisture pooling. The presence of these to factors will likely lead \nto a low chance for unsettled weather in the region Monday. Will \ncontinue to monitor this over the next few model runs. \n\nThe main concern Hurricane Isaias will bring to South Texas will be \nan increased risk of coastal flooding, high rip current risk and \nseas building up to potentially 9-10 feet (confidence is moderate to \nhigh in this occurring over the Gulf waters given the higher \nperiods and northeast winds). A Coastal Flood Advisory will remain\nin effect through Sunday morning. Models remain in agreement that\nswell periods will increase to 9-10 seconds by this after \nafternoon/evening along with the increased seas. This will provide\nsufficient energy to push the water up to the dunes.",
